~ start
Viktor: There it is. The casing is cracked, but the tuner still has power.
Viktor: If I can repair the antenna and find a frequency, I might learn whether anyone is still moving people out of here.
=>< check
Tito: This is a civil defense transmission. The blue evacuation train is still operating toward Belgrade.
Tito: It will follow the main railway line and stop only where boarding is possible.
Tito: Survivors are advised to signal their presence, avoid contaminated ground, and remain clear of unstable slopes.
Viktor: The train is real... and Belgrade is still reachable.
Viktor: She was there when the lines went dead. If I can reach that train, I still have a chance of finding her.
Viktor: I cannot cross the valley blind. I need shelter first, then a route to the railway.
Viktor: That lone tree ahead should break the wind. I will start there.
=> END

~ check
Viktor: First, I need to figure out how to get this working…
- Check the antenna. => wires
- Bang it on the side to see if it works.
	You bang the radio, but nothing happens... Try not to break it, perhaps? => check
- Try to power it on immediately.
	The radio does not start. => check
=> END

~ wires
The antenna still seems sturdy. Exposed wiring crackles in the cold air.
- Twist the wires together barehanded.
	Viktor: Ouch! That hurt. => wires
- Wrap the wires with cloth scraps. (risk of frostbite) => repaired
- Leave wires exposed. => broken
=> END

~ broken
Viktor: It doesn't seem to be working... I should try something else. => check
=> END

~ repaired
Viktor: Managed to insulate the wiring, and… The radio works!
- Turn the dial marked КАЛИБР (calibration).
	Viktor: I think it's callibrated now... => callibrated
- Fiddle with wires randomly.
	Viktor: I'm really.. not doing much..
	Viktor: Maybe I should try to find a signal. => repaired
- Switch to РУЧНО (manual) mode.
	Viktor: Maybe I should callibrate it first... => repaired
=> END

